welcome back to cbs mornings cbs news is  launching a new investigative series  that looks at the disturbing rise in  unsolved murder cases nationwide crime  without punishment is a joint  investigation between cbs stations  across the country and our investigative  team here in new york today in the u.s  there's about a 50-50 chance that a  killer will never be arrested after  taking someone's life especially if the  victim is hispanic or black for police  departments in cities like philadelphia  it's a huge challenge and the first of  three very powerful stories this week  chief investigative correspondent jim  axelrod went inside philadelphia's  homicide unit to see firsthand why it's  so hard to solve murder cases jim good  morning good morning there were more  than 500 homicides in philadelphia last  year and that is more than ever before  and still more than half remain unsolved  we wanted to see why so we visited the  philadelphia police department the  fourth largest in the country  and what we found were detectives  overloaded overwhelmed and wide awake on  a thursday morning at 4 00 a.m  it is just after dawn in philadelphia  this is going to happen pretty fast once  they get out  like they do two to three times every  week detectives from the city's homicide  unit along with a swat team  are raiding a house where they believe a  murder suspect is holed up  they are looking for the man getting out  of this car in this video who they think  then shot and killed an uber driver in a  case of mistaken identity  on this morning the raid is successful  yeah we have two suspects in custody  as they take two men wanted in  connection with the shooting into  custody philadelphia homicide detective  joe murray at this point are you feeling  optimistic so far so good but right now  it's it's we're off to a good start  murray and the five other detectives in  his unit spend each day confronting one  of the most troubling questions in law  enforcement today why do so many murders  now go unsolved and unpunished in  america for us it's the volume  562 homicides were committed in  philadelphia last year  that's about 11 each week it's the  number of murders and we just keep going  and going going it's just  the volume just comes in and it just  crushes you  in the 1960s police solved eight out of  every 10 murder cases in america today  it's barely one in two  nationwide it's  it's a 50 50 coin flip thomas hargrove  runs the murder accountability project a  non-profit that tracks the homicide  clearance rate that's how often the  police solve murder cases it has never  been this bad during the last seven  months of 2020  most murders went unsolved and that's  never happened before in america and  today everything changed technology is  the key as philadelphia's chief  inspector frank fenor told us more than  just gum shoe detectives combing a crime  scene for shell casings and button  holding witnesses just about every  murder has sometimes dozens of sources  of video these days the focus of the  first 48 hours after homicide the most  crucial stretch for investigators  is gathering video videos become our new  witness  [Music]  we visited philadelphia five days after  a shooting on south street a bustling  commercial center left three dead and 11  wounded the area is blanketed by video  surveillance during that time lead  investigator captain jason smith's team  gathered no less than 45 angles of the  shooting but this modern-day dragnet of  sweeping social media and canvassing  businesses for video  is time-consuming often requiring  warrants and subpoenas but it works  while they were showing us footage from  the south street shooting detectives got  word of two more arrests they're in  custody you just got word  just got word right now you feel that  the arrests were made so quickly because  of the video that you have without a  doubt  but for all the technology it's no match  for what cops and chiefs told our  investigative teams at cbs stations  across the country what hinders murder  investigations most is the breakdown  between police and communities of color  in a post-george floyd world there's a  trust factor that we lost along the way  and we try every day to build that trust  factor back up our investigative data  team analyzed unsolved murders across  the country  the rate of arrests over the last 30  years when the victim is white has risen  above 85 percent  watch how the rate declines when the  victim is hispanic  and even fewer arrests are made  when the victim is black  the disparity is stunning it is stunning  it's not just here it's not just here  danielle outlaw is the commissioner of  the philadelphia police department this  disparity isn't five or ten points no  this disparity is gaping yes  are black murders pursued as  aggressively yes as urgently yes with  the same amount of manchester  yes  again  why do you end up with this kind of  disparity it's obvious i'm a black woman  and i run the fourth largest police  department in the country what we need  is a collaborative effort in solving  these cases and an acknowledgement what  i think is there now  that it's going to take more than the  police  to clear these cases and sometimes it  sounds like  what's implicit is that the community of  color  needs to trust the police more no not at  all we're talking about historically  issues systemic inequities that  contribute to the mistrust and then also  things that the police have done and  we've gotten in our own way it has to be  a two-way street as it is with any  relationship  just an arrest is enough to move a  murder case into the cleared column even  if the suspect still awaits a hearing as  this man now is  this counts as a good day for the  beleaguered detectives in philadelphia  who don't have time to examine the  trends  they're too busy trying to reverse them  and tomorrow we're going to report on  the toll unsolved murders take on a  community we visit with a group of  mothers in jackson mississippi who know  the crushing pain of losing their child  and are struggling to find justice  you know jim when commissioner outlaw  says she understands these historical  issues systemic inequities that  contribute to the mistrust they  understand the issue the question is how  do you fix it going forward right her  point being uh and i what i was pressing  her on was why do you hear the community  has to do better the community has to do  better isn't it a question of the  community and the police understanding  the cooperation that's having that trust  that's right and so her point was yes i  agree it is not just the community of  color that needs to do better to solve  to elevate the rate of clearing murder  cases the police have to do better as  well yeah but it's hard when either side  trusts the other though and but there  are bruised feelings on both sides it's  very tough but it's tough to see the  disparity in black and white the way you  just showed it to us sure thank you jim
 

